KG42.register("source_edge_archive-c448314b20763aacce8e4212",{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::E::v141-c-R002-E001","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","rule_ref":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"bidding.supply","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C001"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-b9841ac99d9d29b8","target":"COMP-b3f649e90c1f17b9","source_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::bidding.demand","target_occurrence_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002::V::bidding.supply","source_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::bidding.demand","target_unit_id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::bidding.supply","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"94bf2170604b07fbad852789ff10594f1d52062a25827ca398342e142ea984fe","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-M01","source_definition":"The trading partnership set restricts demand proposals: the bilateral quantity must be zero for a counterparty outside that set. Scope: Buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: When a prospective counterparty is outside the requesting agent's trading partnership set. Current bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its requested energy quantities. Scope: Buying-side quantity updates in the decentralized consensus-ADMM negotiation of (6). Condition: At each negotiation iteration. Participants update requested quantities through their own optimization (6a) and individually send the resulting trade proposals to their trading partners; the negotiation process repeats until convergence. Scope: Submission of buying-side trade proposals in the decentralized P2P negotiation. Condition: During each iteration of the negotiation process, until convergence. Requested quantities must be updated consistently with the agent's total-power bounds, with total power equal to the sum of its bilateral proposals. Scope: Requested-power updates in (6a), jointly with the agent's other bilateral proposals. Condition: At every negotiation iteration. Each prosumer's requested bilateral quantity must remain within that prosumer's power boundaries. Scope: Individual buying-side bilateral proposals in the update (6a). Condition: When the requesting agent is a prosumer. The prescribed update prohibits buying requests from generators by constraining their bilateral proposed quantities to be nonnegative. Scope: Bilateral quantity proposals produced by the update (6a). Condition: When the updating agent is a generator.","target_definition":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^{k}-p_{mn}^{k}}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^{k}-p_{mn}^{k}}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","v141-c-R002-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"bidding.supply","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"paper_scope_note":"The bilateral negotiated price update and the exogenous network fee signal are separately limited in meaning; partner-set constraints, incentive intent, and ADMM computational dependencies cannot be transmitted in a mixed way."}});
